Subject: Keep opcache's shared memory usable under Fil-C
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

Fil-C tracks pointer capabilities per process. Opcache stores compiled scripts,
pointers included, in one MAP_SHARED segment that FPM and CGI workers share, so
a worker that read an entry another worker had cached trapped on a pointer
without a capability. Map the segment privately instead: each worker inherits
what the parent cached before forking (interned strings, preloading) and
caches the rest itself. opcache_reset() and opcache_invalidate() then affect
only the calling process; timestamp validation still sees changed files in
every process. Single-process SAPIs (CLI, the built-in server) are unaffected.

To get huge pages below 4 GiB, opcache maps a region, unmaps it and maps it
again with MAP_FIXED at the old address. Fil-C refuses to reuse the pointer to
the unmapped region, so every SAPI with opcache enabled died at startup. Take
the ordinary MAP_HUGETLB and plain mappings instead.

--- a/ext/opcache/shared_alloc_mmap.c
+++ b/ext/opcache/shared_alloc_mmap.c
@@ -44,6 +44,14 @@
 #if defined(MAP_ANON) && !defined(MAP_ANONYMOUS)
 # define MAP_ANONYMOUS MAP_ANON
 #endif
+#ifdef __FILC__
+/* Fil-C keeps pointer capabilities per process, so a pointer one process
+ * stores in a shared mapping has none in another: an FPM worker trapped
+ * reading the hash entry of a script another worker had cached. Map the
+ * cache privately; forked workers still start from what the parent cached. */
+# undef MAP_SHARED
+# define MAP_SHARED MAP_PRIVATE
+#endif
 #if defined(MAP_ALIGNED_SUPER)
 # include <sys/types.h>
 # include <sys/sysctl.h>
@@ -241,7 +249,7 @@
 	 * (boot time config only, but enabled by default on most arches).
 	 */
 	if (requested_size >= huge_page_size && requested_size % huge_page_size == 0) {
-# if defined(__x86_64__) && defined(MAP_32BIT)
+# if defined(__x86_64__) && defined(MAP_32BIT) && !defined(__FILC__)
 		/* to got HUGE PAGES in low 32-bit address we have to reserve address
 		   space and then remap it using MAP_HUGETLB */
 
